Apple's Mac Studio with the ultra chip  is it worth the money six months later  today we find out by breaking down all  of the issues that I've experienced over  the last six months reviewing who should  buy the Mac Studio with the ultra chip  versus who should buy it with the max  chip who really needs that Ultra why I  personally bought the ultra and finally  of course is it worth the price tag  what's going on guys my name is David  tomich I'm a registered architect here  in Western Australia and today we're  going to be looking at the Mac Studio  Ultra through six months of user  experience now this is my daily driver I  have a Max version at the office as well  so I have a good comparison on both of  these devices so let's get started by  looking at the issues I've had over the  last six months with this device one of  the biggest issues I had was the Mac  Studio and the studio display decided to  stop talking to each other first they  wouldn't produce any sound through the  actual display then the display wouldn't  work at all so this kind of freaked me  out because it was quite early on into  the user experience audience I had to do  quite a bit of Googling on my phone to  actually understand how I could fix this  because I couldn't use the computer  itself to understand what was wrong  eventually what I had to do was unplug  the display from the studio and fully  de-power both systems then reboot as a  hard reset to actually be able to turn  them back on Apple has actually  identified that this was a problem early  on and released a software update for  both the mac and the studio so this  hasn't happened ever since I also found  at the start that everything was still  running on Rosetta 2 and even though  Apple said it would run perfectly fine  there was so many problems I'll use twin  motion as an example for those of you  who don't know twin motion is a  three-day real-time rendering software  for architecture at the start it would  crash continuously I'd use it for about  five minutes and it would crash it  wouldn't just crash the program it would  crash the whole computer you would have  to reboot the entire system to be able  to bring everything back online so for  the first two to three months of owning  the Mac Studio this this was the biggest  burden now the programs that we're still  running on Rosetta 2 at the early days  and still probably are running today  actually ran significantly better which  was a huge Improvement but the problem  was they were still all running on a  rosetta 2. they weren't native they  weren't using the full power of the M1  chips and they were just kind of a  little bit better for the price tag a  little bit better isn't really worth it  so it took a long time for anybody to  even start introducing Apple silicon  native programs and many of the programs  that I still use today including  archicad which I use every single day is  not available for Apple silicon natively  well this brings me to my next point  should you buy that Mac Studio with the  ultra chip or should you buy it with the  max chip well funnily enough I purchased  myself the ultra version and for  everybody in the office I purchased the  max chip now with very good reason and  justification myself personally I plan  on keeping this computer for eight to  ten years which means I'm going to get a  very long lifetime out of this device  whereas from a commercial point of view  we only keep our devices for about five  years until they can no longer keep up  with the demand of the office the main  justification of why we purchased the  max instead of the ultra for the office  is simply for the fact everything is  still running on Rosetta 2 and will  continue to run on Rosetta 2 for a good  two to three years from now which means  if you think about it best case scenario  everything transitions to Apple silicon  maybe 2024 2025 that leaves us with only  two years of Peak Performance on those  devices meaning we've just spent  thousands and thousands of dollars for a  computer that will only really give us  two years worth of its best ability  whereas for me because I plan on keeping  it for significantly longer that means I  do get an extra six years of Peak  Performance out of this device when  everything does transition to Apple  silicon I think being an early adopter  into the Apple silicon chipset was you  know hit and miss a little bit because  you got the added benefits of the speed  performance but not a hundred percent of  what it could actually achieve so even  now six months later with the ultra it  still isn't showing its true colors on  all programs but when it does and when  it truly accelerates and becomes fully  native across everything that you can  imagine who actually needs the M1 Ultra  or potentially the M2 Ultra in the near  future well the answer is actually quite  simple if you're a creative working on  large projects you need the ultra it's  that simple the ultra can handle larger  files larger file types and it can  handle a larger diversity of programs  running simultaneously now if you're a  medium-sized Creator then you can  definitely get away with the max version  even if you're running some small files  some Photoshop some illustrator things  like that Apple silicon with the max  chip will be completely fine but once  you get into that high level of  detailing let's say you're designing  Apartments giant skyscrapers you're  probably going to need something like  the ultra if not something even more  powerful and by creators and creatives I  don't just mean Architects I mean  Architects designers Engineers creators  of All Sorts even if you're a graphic  designer an individual artist a  photographer a videographer all of these  fall under my creative category anybody  that isn't literally just working on  Word Excel and Outlook then you need  something powerful if you are running  just word excel Outlook and you know  general office work you probably don't  need anything above the typical M1 chip  you can get away with the base model of  what apple offers so to wrap this up is  it actually worth the price tag the  simple answer is absolutely yes the  Apple Studio with the ultra chipset is  phenomenal now that the market is  starting to catch up to the actual  Hardware it was a very long time coming  working on Rosetta 2 and slowly  transitioning in to the native apps but  as they've transitioned you can see huge  performance improvements across the  entire system last week I actually  tested rcad 26's Apple silicon beta  version on the device and it ran  significantly better it was producing  numbers that I never thought would be  possible by simply transitioning from a  native Intel or running on Rosetta 2 all  the way to Apple silicon so the numbers  are incredibly impressive once it does  transition and because it has been six  months now and well over a year since  Apple silicon has been introduced to the  world the market has caught up and will  continue to catch up over the next six  months over the next 12 months if you're  looking to buy the Mac Studio with the  ultra chip and you are a creator with  large files I definitely recommend it  for you anyway that's all for me today  guys thank you so so much for watching I  hope you enjoyed the video if you did  make sure you check out the playlist to  the side of me for more great  architectural and Tech content if you  loved the video make sure you smash that  subscribe button down below as well as  the like button for the YouTube  algorithm and like always I'll see you  next Monday
 

